    <DataNode elementId="Fructans" textLabel="a fructan" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="C01355" dataSource="KEGG Compound" />
      <Graphics centerX="350" centerY="400" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment>Fructans are a class of highly water-soluble polysaccharides consisting of linear or branched |FRAME:FRU| chains attached to |FRAME: SUCROSE| by &amp;beta;-2,1or &amp;beta;-2,6-linkage. There are 3 types of fructans: 1. |FRAME: Inulin| - linear fructans generally linked by &amp;beta;(2-&gt;1) glycosidic bonds 2. |FRAME: Levan| - linear fructans generally linked by &amp;beta;(2-&gt;6) glycosidic bonds 3. |FRAME: Graminans| - branched fructans linked by both &amp;beta;(2-&gt;1) and &amp;beta;(2-&gt;6) glycosidic bonds. The fructosyl units may be linked to the sucrose base unit either to the fructose moiety, or to the glucose moiety by a &amp;beta; 2-&gt;6 bond. In the latter case, the resulting molecule name is marked by a 6&lt;sub&gt;G&lt;/sub&gt;, as in |FRAME: NEOKESTOSE 6&lt;sub&gt;G&lt;/sub&gt;-kestotriose|.</Comment>
